.TH XTFETCH 1
.SH NAME
xtfetch \- the machine at a glance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xtfetch
[
.B \-m
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xtfetch
draws a PC/XT on the left and prints beside it what the machine is and
has: the account and the machine's name, the operating system, the
processor (an Intel 8088 or an NEC V20), the 8087, the memory in use,
the root file system's space, the display, the time up, the users
logged in, the shell, and the Development System's version.
.PP
On the console with a color display (TERM ansi) it is in color; with
.B \-m
it is not, for other terminals.
.PP
The processor is told by AAD: an 8088 multiplies by the instruction's
second byte, a V20 always by 10.
.PP
.B xtfetch
is
.BR xtop (1)
under another name, and reads the kernel the same way.
.SH SEE ALSO
xtop(1), uptime(1), df(1)
.SH HISTORY
Written for xenix86-devsys. The idea is neofetch's, by Dylan Araps,
and the reading of the system in C, not in a shell, is fastfetch's. The
PC/XT drawn on the left was designed for xenix86-devsys by Alexander
Akilov.
